    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. but sometimes it feels like the plateau isn't even about physical fatigue, more like just a sudden wall they hit out of nowhere. is there a specific physiological reason why progress just... stops like that? like what's actually happening in the body or the nervous system when that happens?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A plateau can indeed sometimes seem like hitting an insurmountable wall. Physiologically, strength gains are partly reliant on neurological adaptations—the nervous system learning more effective motor patterns for a given movement. As strength training progresses, the rate at which your client's nervous system can adapt may slow down. Additionally, at higher training intensities, the body's feedback mechanism—the central governor—might step in to prevent overexertion as a protective measure, reflecting an unwarranted limit to performance. Furthermore, minor imbalances or compensations developed over time during squat execution can prevent the lifter from achieving perfect form or full range of motion, effectively capping progress despite physical readiness. Taking steps to potentially shift muscle balance, correct form, or rest adequately can assist in overcoming this often nuance-embedded wall.",
      "source": "target",
